cff-version: 1.2.0

message: "If you use these PRISM-games models or verification artifacts, please cite this software record."

title: "Design and Verification of Adaptive Explainability Policies in Service Robots under Uncertainty: PRISM-games Models and Verification Artifacts"

type: software

authors:
  - family-names: "Alharbi"
    given-names: "Mohammed Naji"
    affiliation: "College of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Jeddah"

doi: 10.5281/zenodo.20273873

repository-code: "https://github.com/malharbi2016/formal-adaptive-explainability"

license: Apache-2.0

abstract: >-
  PRISM-games models and verification artifacts supporting a
  preference-parameterized adaptive explainability framework for service
  robots under uncertainty. The implementation includes an adaptive model
  in which the continuous information-preference parameter w_info controls
  the probability of selecting minimal or comprehensive explanations, and
  a non-adaptive randomized baseline using equal explanation-selection
  probabilities. The artifacts support formal evaluation of expected
  cumulative satisfaction-based utility, explanation cost, helpful-response
  probability, and satisfaction probability.

keywords:
  - "Probabilistic Model Checking"
  - "PRISM-games"
  - "Adaptive Explainability"
  - "Explainable Robotics"
  - "Stochastic Multi-Player Games"
  - "Service Robots"
  - "User-Preference Modeling"
  - "Formal Verification"
  - "Human-Robot Interaction"
  - "Reproducibility"
